WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.620
- You guys, like I was
talking about before,

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:03.480
you have such intense scenes together.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.180
So what did you do to keep things light

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.050
while making it so you just
weren't totally depressed?

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:14.038
- I think I binged every
season of Sex and the City

00:00:14.038 --> 00:00:15.060
(Kelly laughing)

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.170
and the movies.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.160
- You went opposite.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.500
- Yeah, I got through all of
them, and then I finished it,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.450
and I still had like three
weeks left of shooting.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.543
So I was like, "What am I
gonna do?" But yeah, that-

00:00:23.543 --> 00:00:24.990
- 'Cause y'all shoot this
during, like, Covid time?

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.190
So you were, like, alone?

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.440
- Yeah, I was completely alone,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.470
and most of the time I was
just interacting with anybody

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:34.689
it was on set, and usually
screaming at Brendan.

00:00:34.689 --> 00:00:37.560
- (laughs) Those were our scenes.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:38.880
The movie is really about, though,

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.940
being honest with, and not
running from, ourselves.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.700
And there's a line that's
repeated from an essay

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:45.817
on "Moby Dick."

00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:48.990
"I felt saddest of all when
I read the boring chapters

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:52.380
that were only descriptions
of whales because I knew

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.360
that the author was just trying to save us

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.690
from his own sad story
just for a little while."

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.930
It's, like, the saddest line ever,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.600
but also rings very true
for a lot of people,

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:04.830
and it hits home.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:08.010
So, like, Brendan, what
have you had to learn

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:09.360
to embrace about yourself?

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.920
Like, did not working
for a while change you?

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.000
- In a way. I'll get there in a sec.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.220
Just to put context to what you read,

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:21.630
that's from the essay that
Sadie's character Ellie

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:24.540
has written when she's
about eight years old.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.570
And because Charlie is an English teacher

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.480
he believes this to be the best essay

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.480
he's ever read because
of how honest it is.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.810
And he implores his students,
who he teaches online,

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.403
with the camera turned off,

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.560
to do the same just give him
something honest to read.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.110
And once she does,

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.050
it's almost as if it sets
their relationship free.

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.240
- Yeah. I love that. I
didn't wanna spoiler alert,

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.500
that's why I didn't say who did it.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.640
But you did.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.104
(audience and Sadie laughing)

00:01:55.104 --> 00:01:56.977
I literally asked my executive
producer, I was like,

00:01:56.977 --> 00:01:59.881
"Am I allowed to say it's her
or will that spoil anything?"

00:01:59.881 --> 00:02:02.160
- Brendan, you gave it away.
- But Brendan did it, not me.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.000
Um, so it's, like, an interesting thing.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.331
You go through the film,

00:02:07.331 --> 00:02:09.360
and it's something that you prize,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.770
and then you find out why.

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.810
And I think, like, what you
just said about it, too,

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.070
towards the end, I'm glad you said it

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:18.360
because it is freeing to be
honest even, like, if you're sad

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:19.193
or even if-

00:02:19.193 --> 00:02:20.760
That's real, like, we get sad.

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.860
We go through things,
and it's a real thing,

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:24.600
and a raw thing to be.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.500
If you're gonna be a writer,

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.700
and you're gonna write something,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.748
might as well be pure
right? So I agree with that.

00:02:29.748 --> 00:02:30.930
(audience applauding)

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:35.040
But Brendan, you got a six
minute standing ovation

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.340
when "The Whale" premiered
at the Venice Film Festival.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.110
How did that feel?

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:43.083
I mean, you seemed so overwhelmed.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:45.413
Yeah.

00:02:45.413 --> 00:02:46.246
- Correct!

00:02:46.246 --> 00:02:48.990
- Yeah. It had to be just so overwhelming,

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.613
and that role is so important.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:56.870
- Really affirmed, surprised, astonished,

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.354
and a little bit Italian.

00:03:00.354 --> 00:03:02.680
(audience, Sadie, and Kelly laughing)

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:06.697
- What? Oh my god, you're
always welcome on this show.

00:03:06.697 --> 00:03:09.462
(audience cheering)
(Sadie clapping)

00:03:09.462 --> 00:03:12.810
Oh, what, instead of asking him

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:14.340
because he was obviously exquisite,

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:15.780
what was it like to watch?

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.850
'Cause you've been shooting
this movie with him

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.470
and going through these very
intense scenes, like you said,

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.240
you're like always yelling
at him and you're, you know,

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:25.020
very angry. Like, what was
it like to watch that happen?

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.520
- I was a little nervous.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.210
That was the first time we'd
showed it to an audience.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.340
So I think they were all a little relieved

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.440
that they were clapping and standing and-

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:35.310
- [Kelly] Responded so-

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.871
- [Sadie] Responded, yeah.

00:03:36.871 --> 00:03:38.220
- Oh, that's intense.

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.690
I didn't realize it was the first time.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.910
- Yeah, it's very nerve-racking,

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:42.960
but it turned out well.
- Oh, God,

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.840
I would never want to experience
that for the first time

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.960
in front of a ton of people. That's brave.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:53.430
So Brendan, before this movie
do you remember the last

00:03:53.430 --> 00:03:54.690
trophy you ever won?

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:55.713
Like the last thing?

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.071
- I remember one of the very first times.

00:04:00.071 --> 00:04:00.904
- What?

00:04:00.904 --> 00:04:02.580
I was in grade four,

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.270
and I knew there was a banquet coming up,

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:10.770
and I was excited because
the season had ended,

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:14.430
and they were handing
out awards and prizes.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:19.320
And I was waiting for them to
call my name, and they did!

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.777
It was for the peewee bowling league.

00:04:21.777 --> 00:04:24.328
(Kelly and audience laughing)

00:04:24.328 --> 00:04:25.419
(audience, Sadie, and Kelly clapping)

00:04:25.419 --> 00:04:27.217
- Yes!

00:04:27.217 --> 00:04:28.467
I love bowling.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:32.340
- I think it was probably
a participation trophy,

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:34.320
and they did misspell my name on it.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:37.223
I think it said like Borton
Kushner or something like that.

00:04:37.223 --> 00:04:39.284
(audience and Sadie laughing)

00:04:39.284 --> 00:04:40.117
- Oh that's so sad.
- But you know what,

00:04:40.117 --> 00:04:43.143
It was my bowling trophy,
and I was very proud of it.

00:04:43.143 --> 00:04:45.082
(audience and Sadie clapping)

00:04:45.082 --> 00:04:48.180
- So, okay, so the
difference, I do have to say,

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:50.160
and maybe you remember
this, you were young 10,

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:54.600
but in Texas they don't give
you participation ribbons.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:56.820
You either win or lose.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:59.580
It's like, no, but I think
it so you got sad about it

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:00.903
'cause we're here in LA.

00:05:02.310 --> 00:05:04.200
I know, but I'm just saying
I think it's important

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.160
to learn how to lose.
- [Brendan] Does anyone know

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:05.993
what that means?

00:05:05.993 --> 00:05:06.826
- What did you say?

00:05:06.826 --> 00:05:09.090
- I said it was for a high game handicap,

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.518
and to this day I still
don't know what that means.

00:05:11.518 --> 00:05:13.226
(audience and Kelly laughing)

00:05:13.226 --> 00:05:14.610
- I don't know what that means either,

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:18.250
but I don't think it's good. I don't know.

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:21.270
Okay. Do you still have that trophy?

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:22.890
I was wondering if we could see it.

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:25.818
- Sadly, no. It's lost
to the sands of time.

00:05:25.818 --> 00:05:27.330
- Oh, that's such a bummer.

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:29.490
Well, we heard about
the lost bowling trophy,

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:30.750
so we sent you a little gift.

00:05:30.750 --> 00:05:31.620
It should be there.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.660
So go ahead and open it.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:34.493
- What?
- Yep.

00:05:35.728 --> 00:05:37.071
(audience cheering)
(Sadie clapping)

00:05:37.071 --> 00:05:38.238
- Here?
- Yep.

00:05:40.047 --> 00:05:40.880
Yes. Look at that! Look at that!

00:05:42.991 --> 00:05:47.034
(clapping) It's about
to get real exciting.

00:05:47.034 --> 00:05:48.090
- No way!

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:51.090
(audience cheering)

00:05:52.433 --> 00:05:53.562
- Brian Fusher.

00:05:53.562 --> 00:05:57.440
(audience, Sadie, and Kelly clapping)

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.160
We just kinda made up a name

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.580
'cause we know it didn't
say yours anyway. (laughs)

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:06.159
- And I'd like to thank
the makers of bowling balls

00:06:06.159 --> 00:06:08.847
and bowling pins.

00:06:08.847 --> 00:06:13.325
And the wax that makes the aisle so shiny.

00:06:13.325 --> 00:06:17.357
The shoes! I love you smelly
shoes that are rented!

00:06:18.936 --> 00:06:21.186
- Oh, it says Brian Fusher.

00:06:22.692 --> 00:06:23.910
So we engraved it for you.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:25.297
Do you mind reading it?

00:06:25.297 --> 00:06:28.111
- "Just give me something (beep) honest."

00:06:28.111 --> 00:06:32.996
(audience and Kelly clapping)
(Kelly and Sadie laughing)

00:06:32.996 --> 00:06:35.343
- Thank you!

00:06:36.431 --> 00:06:37.870
Thank you, Brendan!

